通过GPU微架构分析，揭示多标量乘法中的寄存器压力与指令级并行度不足等性能瓶颈，并设计针对性的计算优化方法；其次，通过受存储容量约束的递归分块与数据布局优化，实现大规模数论变换的高效执行；最后，基于证明生成流程的数据依赖关系，协同调度磁盘、主存与显存之间的数据传输及计算任务，降低存储需求与流水线等待开销。实验结果表明，LightZK在常规规模下，相较领先的可运行GPU基线实现取得了2.8倍的端到端加速，并能够在单节点上完成2³⁰（约十亿）规模约束的证明生成，展现出良好的性能与可扩展性，为大规模零知识证明的实际应用提供了高效的计算支撑。\nL77: ACM Transactions on Architecture and Code Optimization（TACO）是计算机系统结构和编译领域的权威国际学术期刊，也是中国计算机学会CCF A类期刊。\nL78: \nL79: （通讯员：卢必然）\nL80: \nL81: ﻿\nL82: \nL83: 地址:武汉市洪山区珞瑜路1037号, 华中科技大学东五楼2楼　邮编: 430074 电话: 15387112483\nL84: 计算机科学与技术学院，大数据技术与系统国家地方联合工程研究中心、服务计算技术与系统教育部重点实验室、集群与网格计算湖北省重点实验室\nL85: 有问题和意见请与网站管理员联系 yukundu@yeah.net   总访问人次：\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNTT | 4-Step Algorithm (https://zprize.hardcaml.com/ntt-4step.html)\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://zprize.hardcaml.com/ntt-4step.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 67\nL0:   * 【0†MSM】\nL1:     * 【1†MSM overview】\nL2:     * 【2†Design Overview】\nL3:       * 【3†Top-Level Pippenger Design】\nL4:       * 【4†Pippenger Controller】\nL5:       * 【5†Field Multiplication】\nL6:       * 【6†Twisted Edwards Point Representation】\nL7:       * 【7†Mixed Point Addition with Precomputation】\nL8:       * 【8†Scalar Transformation】\nL9:       * 【9†Implementation Details】\nL10:       * 【10†Host driver software】\nL11:       * 【11†Results】\nL12:       * 【12†Building, Testing and Benchmarking】\nL13:       * 【13†Future Work】\nL14:     * 【14†Hardcaml on the Web】\nL15:       * 【15†Karatsuba ofman multiplier】\nL16:       * 【16†Barrett Reduction】\nL17:       * 【17†Top level design as a Vitis kernel】\nL18:   * 【18†NTT】\nL19:     * 【19†NTT Overview】\nL20:     * 【20†Design Overviews】\nL21:       * 【21†4-Step Algorithm】\nL22:       * 【22†Memory Bandwidth】\nL23:       * 【23†Core NTT design】\nL24:       * 【24†Performance Scaling】\nL25:       * 【25†Top-Level Hardcaml Design】\nL26:       * 【26†Results】\nL27:       * 【27†Build Instructions】\nL28:     * 【28†Hardcaml on the Web】\nL29:       * 【29†NTT Single Core Hardware Design】\nL30:       * 【30†Top level design as a Vitis kernel】\nL31: # 4-Step Algorithm\nL32: \nL33: The core design limits us to transform sizes which can fit within on-chip FPGA RAM resources. To break this limit we use the well known 4-step algorithm as described in 【31†this paper†arxiv.org】. An OCaml reference implementation is provided in our 【32†repository†github.com】.\nL34: \nL35: The idea is to break the large transform into lots of smaller transforms. Let’s consider our target transform size of $2^24$. We first reformulate the input data as a $2^12 x 2^12$ matrix in row major order.\nL36: The following steps are then performed:\nL37: \nL38:   1. Perform $2^12$ NTTs on each column.\nL39:   2. Multiply each element of the matrix at location $(i,j)$ by $ω^(i.j)$, where $ω$ is the appropriate root of unity of the transform size.\nL40:   3. Perform $2^12$ NTTs on each row.\nL41:   4. Transpose the result.\nL42: \nL43: The required NTT transform can now easily fit within on-chip FPGA RAM resources, so long as we can store the complete matrix in external memory and access it efficiently.\nL44: ## Performance\nL45: \nL46: Using the 【33†Cooley-Tukey FFT algorithm†en.wikipedia.org】, a transform of size $2^24$ requires $2^24 log_{2} 2^24 = 402,653,184$ operations.\nL47: The 4-step algorithm performs $2 . 2^12$ smaller NTT transforms, each of size $2^12$. This works out to the same number of $402,653,184$ operations as directly computing a $2^24$ transform. However, we must add to this a further $2 . 2^24$ operations to perform the scaling operation (the scaling requires 2 multiplications—one to scale the coefficient, the other to update the scaling factor).\nL48: \nL49: The following table gives the total operations and overhead for a few transform sizes.\nL50: $log_{2} N$ NTT  | Full transform operations  | 4-step operations  | Overhead %\nL51: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL52: 18  | 4718592  | 5242880  | 11.1\nL53: 20  | 20971520  | 23068672  | 10.0\nL54: 22  | 92274688  | 100663296  | 9.0\nL55: 24  | 402653184  | 436207616  | 8.3\nL56: 26  | 1744830464  | 1879048192  | 7.7\nL57: 28  | 7516192768  | 8053063680  | 7.1\nL58: ## Transposition\nL59: \nL60: The 4-step algorithm talks about row and column transforms and a final transposition step. This is not actually performed by the hardware design. Rather data is read and written from the matrix in memory as follows:\nL61: \nL62:   1. Read columns\nL63:   2. Write columns\nL64:   3. Read rows\nL65:   4. Write columns\nL66: Reading a single column would be very inefficient for DDR-style dynamic memories. Fortunately as we 【24†scale up】 the design for performance we can read multiple columns at a time. We will also show a 【22†data reorganisation approach】 which leads to very efficient memory access at the cost of reordering the coefficients in the input matrix.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - Alisah-Ozcan/GPU-NTT: Welcome to the GPU-NTT-Optimization repository! We present cutting-edge algorithms and implementations for optimizing the Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) on Graphics Processing Units (GPUs). · GitHub (https://github.com/Alisah-Ozcan/GPU-NTT)\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Alisah-Ozcan/GPU-NTT\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 377\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       * 【6†CodespacesInstant dev environments】\nL17:       * 【7†IssuesPlan and track work】\nL18:   